{
  "result": "At the edge of a town, there was a library that opened before sunrise. The librarian, Mara, said that books deserved to see the morning, even if nobody came to read them. Each day she unlocked the door, switched on a lamp, and placed a different book on the windowsill. Some were novels. Some were field guides. One was a cookbook with a faded picture of a pear.\n\nMost mornings, the street remained empty. Then a baker began pausing on his way home from work. He never entered, but he studied each cover as though it were a message addressed to him.\n\nOne rainy Tuesday, he found a book about birds. That afternoon, he returned with a paper bag containing two warm rolls. Mara offered him a chair, and he told her about the swallows nesting beneath his shop\u2019s awning.\n\nSoon other people noticed the lamp. A bus driver stopped in for poetry. A girl borrowed an atlas and traced routes to places she might never visit. Someone left a pot of yellow flowers by the door.\n\nThe library did not become famous. Its roof still leaked, and its clock was usually seven minutes slow. But the early light made a small promise: there was room here for curiosity, for company, and for beginnings that did not need to announce themselves.\n\nAnd every morning, Mara chose another book, set it in the window, and waited while the town slowly learned to look up. Sometimes, that was enough to change everything.",
